berkeley-principles-3.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1, to: Suppose "possibly, an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers" is true and "possibly, an idea represents a spirit in the powers of thinking, willing and perceiving" is false. Is S1, "I answer, if it does not in those mentioned, it is impossible it should represent it in any other thing.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
berkeley-principles-3.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1 has "¬◇rep_other"; berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1.3 have "spirit_only_powers → □¬rep_other"; they differ when only "possibly, an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers" is true, which the second allows
berkeley-principles-3.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1, to: Suppose "possibly, an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers" and "possibly, an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers" are true and "by the word spirit we mean only that which thinks, wills and perceives" and "necessarily, it is not so that an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers" are false. Is S2, "Do but leave out the power of willing, thinking, and perceiving ideas, and there remains nothing else wherein the idea can be like a spirit.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1 has "spirit_only_powers → ¬◇rep_other" and "□(idea_spirit → rep_powers ∨ rep_other)"; berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1.2 has "□(idea_spirit → rep_powers ∨ rep_other)"; they differ when only "by the word spirit we mean only that which thinks, wills and perceives", "necessarily, (if there is an idea of a spirit, then (either an idea represents a spirit in the powers of thinking, willing and perceiving or an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers))" and "possibly, an idea represents a spirit in some other thing than those powers" are true, which the second allows; berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1.3 has "¬◇rep_powers" and "□(idea_spirit → rep_powers ∨ rep_other)"
berkeley-principles-3.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1 and berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1.2 have "¬◇rep_powers → ¬◇idea_spirit"; berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1.3 has "¬◇idea_spirit"; they differ when only "possibly, there is an idea of a spirit", "possibly, there is an idea of a spirit" and "possibly, an idea represents a spirit in the powers of thinking, willing and perceiving" are true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-3.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1 and berkeley-principles-3.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "possibly, there is an idea of a spirit", "possibly, there is an idea of a spirit" and "possibly, an idea represents a spirit in the powers of thinking, willing and perceiving" are true and "necessarily, it is not so that there is an idea of a spirit" and "necessarily, it is not so that an idea represents a spirit in the powers of thinking, willing and perceiving" are false. Is the conclusion, "If therefore it is impossible that any degree of those powers should be represented in an idea, it is evident there can be no idea of a spirit.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
